1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,363
In a small county, the health department is a few people.

2
00:00:04,513 --> 00:00:07,590
They answer for every number in the yearly report.

3
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:10,665
I know what my community needs.

4
00:00:10,815 --> 00:00:14,040
Proving it means digging through reports and spreadsheets.

5
00:00:14,190 --> 00:00:16,228
And every year, I start over.

6
00:00:17,428 --> 00:00:20,329
Here is what the yearly numbers usually miss.

7
00:00:20,479 --> 00:00:23,384
Residents look for help. Many get referred.

8
00:00:23,534 --> 00:00:25,423
Far fewer complete the step.

9
00:00:25,573 --> 00:00:27,707
Somewhere on the way, care breaks.

10
00:00:28,807 --> 00:00:31,273
Know where care breaks. Show the proof.

11
00:00:33,373 --> 00:00:37,362
See where residents get stuck, by kind of help, area and season.

12
00:00:39,462 --> 00:00:41,731
See which barriers come up most often.

13
00:00:44,031 --> 00:00:47,516
Small counts are hidden, so no one can be identified.

14
00:00:49,416 --> 00:00:52,860
Every figure names its source, its year and its range.

15
00:00:53,010 --> 00:00:56,267
Missing stays missing. It never becomes a zero.

16
00:00:57,767 --> 00:01:00,680
Each action in your plan links to its evidence.

17
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:06,393
Your yearly report starts from your plan, not a blank page.

18
00:01:06,543 --> 00:01:09,359
Every draft waits for a person to approve it.

19
00:01:10,959 --> 00:01:15,114
Sources shown. Nobody identifiable. People decide.

20
00:01:16,414 --> 00:01:20,447
Want this for your county? Talk to the SozoRock Foundation.
